## MCP server types

`MCPServer` resources support `type: streamable-http` and `type: sse` in a
deployed muster. Both reach the MCP server over HTTP, so the server runs as its
own workload with its own identity and its own RBAC.

`type: stdio` is **CLI-only** and is rejected by this deployment. A stdio server
is started as a subprocess of the muster process, so accepting one here would
let anyone who may write `MCPServer` resources execute code inside the muster
pod as muster's ServiceAccount. muster refuses it in three places:

* `mcpserver_validate`, `mcpserver_create`, and `mcpserver_update` fail the tool
  call with a message naming the remote alternatives.
* a stdio `MCPServer` applied straight through the API server (bypassing the
  aggregator) is reconciled to `status.state: Failed` with the same message,
  and no subprocess is started.
* the service layer refuses to build a stdio client at all.

stdio keeps working when muster runs as a local CLI (`muster serve` against a
config directory), which is what it exists for. To use an existing stdio MCP
server from a deployed muster, run it as a Deployment behind a Service and
register it with `type: streamable-http`.

## Who should hold mcpserver-editor and workflow-editor

Session-initiated writes to `MCPServer` and `Workflow` resources go through the
Kubernetes API **as the calling user** (writes-as-caller), so Kubernetes RBAC is
the authorization gate. The chart ships that gate as two namespaced Role +
RoleBinding pairs in muster's install namespace:

* `mcpserver-editor` — create/update/patch/delete on `mcpservers`, and
  create (only) on `secrets` for a connector's client entered in the portal
  (`rbac.mcpServerEditor`); the `mcpServerClientSecrets` policy holds that
  create to such client Secrets for a writer who may not get Secrets
* `workflow-editor` — create/update/patch/delete on `workflows`
  (`rbac.workflowEditor`)

These grants are wider than they read, because the aggregator is **shared**:
a registered MCP server is visible to every user of the instance, and other
users' sessions are routed through it. A workflow authored by one user is
listed for and runnable by others.

Pick the bindings' subjects to match how the instance is used:

* **Shared / multi-team installs** should narrow `subjects` to an admin group,
  so only platform administrators curate what everyone else is exposed to.
* **Single-team installs**, where every authenticated SSO user is trusted to
  register servers and author workflows, may bind `system:authenticated`.

Whoever holds `mcpserver-editor` is a **connector admin**: they set a
connector's tool approval (`spec.approval`) and may let an agent skip a tool's
`Ask` (see [tool approval](../../docs/how-to/mcp-server-management.md#tool-approval); a platform
built on muster may describe it further, for example the Giant Swarm Agent Platform's
[Governing tool calls](https://docs.giantswarm.io/overview/agent-platform/governing-tool-calls/)).
So `rbac.mcpServerEditor.subjects` binds **nobody by default**: the Role ships,
its RoleBinding renders only for a non-empty list. Name the connector admins
there (an admin group, or `system:authenticated` to keep the chart's earlier
"everyone registers" default). **Upgrading** from a chart that bound
`system:authenticated`: with `subjects` empty the render fails while the cluster
still holds the old RoleBinding, naming its subjects; set `subjects`, or
`rbac.mcpServerEditor.allowRemovingBinding: true` to drop it. `workflow-editor` still defaults to
`system:authenticated`, and an empty `rbac.workflowEditor.subjects` **fails the
render**: a RoleBinding bound to nobody looks enforced while granting nothing.
To manage the grants entirely out of band, set
`rbac.mcpServerEditor.create: false` / `rbac.workflowEditor.create: false` and
bind the verbs yourself.

## Who may send approval settings (muster-approval-binder)

A Session binding carries an agent's tool list. muster takes its per-entry
settings, `default` and grant marks only from a sender whose projected
ServiceAccount token (audience muster) passes a `TokenReview` and whose
identity a `LocalSubjectAccessReview` allows verb `bind` on `toolapprovals` in
group `muster.giantswarm.io` in muster's namespace (`muster.namespace`, default
the release namespace). The chart ships that permission as the Role
`muster-approval-binder` (`rbac.approvalBinder`) in that namespace, bound to
nobody by default — the Agent Platform binds it to agent-manager's
ServiceAccount, with a RoleBinding in muster's namespace — and grants muster's
ServiceAccount `create` on `tokenreviews` (a ClusterRole named
`<fullname>-<release namespace>-token-reviewer`: the API is cluster-scoped) and
on `localsubjectaccessreviews` in muster's namespace only
(`rbac.approvalBinder.reviews`, also for an external ServiceAccount). Bind only
agent-manager's ServiceAccount: every subject bound is trusted to send an
agent's settings and grant marks. `rbac.approvalBinder.subjects` binds the Role
here instead.

## Rolling on credential rotation

muster reads its OAuth credentials (the Dex or Google client secret, the registration token, the token
encryption key) and the Valkey password from Secrets projected into the pod at start and never again. The
pod template carries a checksum over them beside `checksum/config`, so a rotated credential restarts muster:

- With `muster.oauth.server.existingSecret` empty the chart renders the Secret `<fullname>-oauth` from the
  values; `checksum/oauth-secret` is the SHA-256 of that Secret's data and follows every change of those
  values, an inline Valkey password included.
- With `muster.oauth.server.existingSecret` set the chart cannot read the Secret; `checksum/oauth-secret` is
  `muster.oauth.server.existingSecretChecksum` verbatim. A Valkey password in a Secret of its own
  (`muster.oauth.server.storage.valkey.existingSecret`) is marked the same way by
  `muster.oauth.server.storage.valkey.existingSecretChecksum`, rendered as `checksum/valkey-secret`. Change the
  mark in the same change that rotates the Secret (a hash over the new data, a counter, a date); a Flux
  `HelmRelease` can feed it from a Secret or ConfigMap key through a `valuesFrom` entry with `targetPath`, so it
  changes with the rotation on its own. While a mark is empty, a rotation of that Secret alone leaves the
  running muster on the old values.

## CRD lifecycle

This chart owns its CRDs. They live in the Helm 3 `crds/` directory, so
`helm install` creates them and `helm upgrade` never touches them again. The
`crds.install` and `crds.annotations` values are an inert shim from an earlier
design and change nothing.

Two supported ways exist to upgrade the CRDs with the app:

* Flux: set `install.crds` and `upgrade.crds` to `CreateReplace` on the muster
  `HelmRelease`. This is the default path on Giant Swarm installations.
* Plain Helm: install the standalone `muster-crds` chart before this one, then
  upgrade it first on every release. Its CRDs are templated, so they upgrade.
  See `helm/muster-crds/README.md`.
